1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,780
Hello everyone! We are Joe Doan, Tony Nguyen, Tina Nguyen, and Salvatore Nigro, advised by Professor Yugyung Lee and Professor Sudipta Chattopadhyay, presenting OptiSched.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:19,552
Universities assign classrooms months before registration opens, so sizing rooms to last term's headcount leaves high-demand courses overflowing.

3
00:00:19,552 --> 00:00:22,737
Let's sign in as Course Coordinator and open Insights.

4
00:00:22,957 --> 00:00:35,345
As shown in the green badge at top right, OptiSched trains on nineteen terms of real University of Illinois registrar records from Fall 2016 to Fall 2025, forecasting one term ahead.

5
00:00:35,565 --> 00:00:45,926
Our champion pooled Ridge model predicts next-term enrollment and calibrates eighty percent and ninety-five percent split-conformal intervals, shown by the blue bars on the right of the chart.

6
00:00:46,146 --> 00:00:52,022
When we select different courses in the dropdown, the historical enrollment curve and prediction intervals update live.

7
00:00:52,242 --> 00:01:08,720
Scrolling down to our held-out Spring 2026 benchmark across forty courses, Stage 1 on the left shows pooled Ridge cuts forecast error to 17.24 seats versus 28.25 for copying Fall 2025, while covering 85 and 97.5 percent of courses.

8
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:21,541
On the right, Stage 2 shows our CP-SAT schedule achieves zero hard conflicts, matches actual UIUC seat utilization at 64.9 percent, and preserves 84 percent of faculty two-year teaching habits.

9
00:01:21,761 --> 00:01:30,911
In the unseated student chart below, copying Fall 2025 leaves 338 students without a seat, and the point forecast leaves 153.

10
00:01:30,911 --> 00:01:38,964
Sizing rooms to OptiSched's eighty percent conformal bound reduces unseated students to 60, a 61 percent drop.

11
00:01:39,184 --> 00:01:48,963
Below the chart, the Data and Evaluation box separates real UIUC FOIA enrollment and teaching history from the simulated room pool and What-If scenarios.

12
00:01:49,183 --> 00:01:59,375
Opening the Master Schedule, CP-SAT certifies zero hard conflicts across all forty sections, and coordinators can switch live between the Room Matrix, Weekly Calendar, and Table View.

13
00:01:59,595 --> 00:02:03,170
Now let's test three live disruptions in the What-If Simulator.

14
00:02:03,170 --> 00:02:08,392
First, an enrollment surge with Computer Science up 20 percent and Statistics up 15 percent.

15
00:02:08,392 --> 00:02:13,783
Clicking Run Simulation re-solves live and moves only over-capacity sections into larger rooms.

16
00:02:14,003 --> 00:02:18,354
Second, an instructor becomes unavailable for an NSF grant course release.

17
00:02:18,354 --> 00:02:26,349
Clicking Preset 2 and running the solver assigns a qualified colleague in the same department to cover STAT 420, moving just one course.

18
00:02:26,569 --> 00:02:31,242
Third, a classroom reassignment scales room capacities down to 0.9 times baseline.

19
00:02:31,242 --> 00:02:38,480
Running the solver relocates only the courses that exceed the reduced room limits while keeping all other placements unchanged.

20
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:47,904
Finally, as an extension for faculty commercializing research, the Commercialization Advisor drafts a UM-16C invention disclosure from a paper abstract.

21
00:02:47,904 --> 00:02:54,807
By connecting calibrated demand intervals to CP-SAT optimization, OptiSched sizes rooms for likely student demand.

22
00:02:54,807 --> 00:02:55,413
Thank you!
